course: dictionary
lesson: 15266
title: Sicken and its meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: One word can have more than one meaning, so look at the words around it to choose the right one. «It sickens me to see people being cruel to animals.» The words cruel and animals tell you that sicken means to shock or disgust someone and make them feel upset.
explain: «The little puppy began to sicken after eating the poison.» Here sicken means to start becoming ill. The clue is poison: something bad has made the puppy unwell.
explain: «The smell from the rubbish bin was sickening.» Sickening is the adjective. It describes something horrible, like a crash, a sound or a smell. Try your meaning in the sentence to check it makes sense.
fact: Sicken is a verb, so its forms are sicken, sickens, sickened and sickening.
fact: To sicken someone means to shock or disgust them, or to make them very upset.
fact: Sicken can also mean to start becoming ill, as in «began to sicken».
fact: Old-fashioned: «sicken of» something means to become tired of it.
fact: In everyday talk, «a sickening goal» is a very disappointing goal for the losing team.
exercise: «The news of the cruel crash sickened everyone who heard it.» What does sickened mean here? | Put them to sleep | Made them laugh | *Shocked and disgusted them | Helped them to heal
exercise: «The lamb began to sicken after drinking from the stream.» What does sicken mean here? | *To start becoming ill | To feel very happy | To grow very large | To become tired of something
exercise: «The sickening smell of smoke filled the room.» Find the word that describes the smell. | room | *sickening | filled | smell
test: «It sickens me that people throw litter in the park.» What does sickens mean? | Makes me feel sleepy | Makes me want to dance | Makes the park smell nice | *Makes me feel disgusted and upset
test: Ella says sicken always means to be ill. Is she right? | Yes, it only ever means ill | Yes, it means to fall asleep | *No, it can also mean to shock or disgust someone | No, it means to win a race
exam: «After months of practice, she sickened of the piano and stopped playing.» In this old-fashioned use, what does sickened of mean? | Felt proud of it | *Became tired of it | Became very rich | Learned to play faster
